✅ 1. Check Server Manager Dashboard (Core Health Overview)
Steps:

Open Server Manager

Look at the Dashboard

Check the following tiles:

Local Server – Errors, warnings, NIC issues

All Servers – Red/Yellow alerts

Services – Any failed or stopped services

Event Viewer Summary – Critical / Error logs

Roles & Features – Check status

Why it's useful:

✔ Shows overall system health
✔ Displays service failures
✔ Shows storage, NIC, firewall, and role issues

✅ 2. Event Viewer (GUI Monitoring)
Steps:

Start → search Event Viewer

Check these critical logs:

Windows Logs → System

Windows Logs → Application

Windows Logs → Security

Applications and Services Logs → HardwareEvents

Look for:

🟥 Critical
🟧 Errors
🟨 Warnings

Why it’s useful:

✔ Shows hardware failures, driver issues, app crashes, service failures.

✅ 3. Resource Monitor (CPU, Disk, Network, Memory Health)
Steps:

Start → search Resource Monitor

Check tabs:

CPU – High usage, stuck processes

Memory – Free/Standby/Used

Disk – High read/write, queues

Network – Latency, dropped packets

Why it’s useful:

✔ Detects if server is slow due to resource congestion.

✅ 4. Task Manager (Real-Time Health)
Steps:

Ctrl + Shift + Esc → Task Manager

Go to Performance tab

Look at:

CPU usage

Memory consumption

Disk usage

Network throughput

Uptime

Why:

✔ Quick performance snapshot
✔ Great for diagnosing high usage.

✅ 5. Services.msc (Check for Failed Critical Services)
Steps:

Run → services.msc

Sort by:

Status

Startup Type

Check if the following services are running:

Windows Update

DNS Server (if applicable)

DHCP Server (if used)

Active Directory services (if domain controller)

SQL Server (if hosting DB)

Why:

✔ Ensures core services are healthy.

✅ 6. Disk Management (Check Disk Health & Space)
Steps:

Run → diskmgmt.msc

Check:

Disk status (Healthy / Failed / Degraded)

Free space

RAID volumes

Disk type (Basic / Dynamic)

Why:

✔ Confirms storage health visually.

✅ 7. Reliability Monitor (Hidden but Powerful GUI Tool)
Steps:

Start → search Reliability Monitor
(Or run: perfmon /rel)

Look at graph of:

System errors

App crashes

Windows failures

Hardware issues

Why:

✔ Shows daily stability index
✔ Great for spotting failing apps/drivers.

✅ 8. Performance Monitor (Detailed Monitoring)
Steps:

Start → Performance Monitor

Add counters like:

Processor → % Processor Time

Memory → Available MBytes

Disk → Avg Disk Queue Length

Network → Packets Received Errors

Why:

✔ Best for long-term performance diagnosis.

✅ 9. Check Windows Update Status
Steps:

Settings → Windows Update

Ensure:

Updates succeeded

No failed updates

Last update date is recent

Why:

✔ Outdated servers often become unstable or vulnerable.

✅ 10. Device Manager (Hardware Issues)
Steps:

Run → devmgmt.msc

Look for:

Yellow triangle warnings

Missing drivers

Failing devices

Why:

✔ Checks hardware & driver health.

